*)Ansible is a configuration management and automation tool used to manage and configure many servers from one place or one server.

*)A control node is the main machine where Ansible is installed, managed nodes are the remote servers that are managed by the control node.

*)Ansible is an agentless tool(No software installation needed on remote servers), which means it performs automation 
  without installing any agent or additional software in managed nodes(remote servers). It uses SSH (Secure Shell) protocol to communicate with remote servers.
  
*)Ansible playbooks are written in YAML format.
  
*)Playbook: A playbook is a collection of one or more plays that contains tasks.

#)YAML:

*)YAML is a simple and data serialization human-readable language used to organize configuration data in a structured way.

Data Serialization:
Serialization means converting data into a format so that it can be stored or sent from one system to another.

#)Indentation: 

*)Indentation is important in YAML because YAML uses spaces to define structure and hierarchy. Incorrect indentation can cause syntax errors and playbook failures.

#)SSH:

*)SSH stands for Secure Shell. It is a secure protocol used to connect and communicate with remote servers over a network.

Playbook → Collection of plays
Play → Defines target hosts and tasks
Task → Single action that will be performed on the managed nodes or remote servers by Ansible.
Module → Performs the actual work

## VERY-IMPORTANT ##) ANSIBLE TASK EXECUTION Flow:
--------------------------------------------------

*)For every task, the control node (Ansible server) transfers the appropriate Ansible module to the managed node (remote server) through SSH,
  then the module executes locally on the managed node, collect the execution result of a module and then sends the structured result(output) to the control node, 
  and then the transferred temporary module is removed from the manage node(remote server).

1. Read the playbook.
↓
2. Parse(Read) the current task.
↓
3. Read the module name mentioned in the task (eg: yum, shell, service, copy...)
↓
4. Load that module from the Ansible module library on the control node (or) Ansible server.
↓
5. Establish an SSH connection to the managed node (or) remote server.
↓
6. Transfer the loaded module to the managed node (or) remote server.
↓
7. Execute the module on the managed node (or) remote server.
↓
8. Collect the execution details and prepare a structured output (dictionary i.e stdout, stderr, rc, changed, failed...)
↓
9. Send output back to the control node (or) Ansible server.
↓
10. Delete the transferred temporary module (eg: yum, shell, service, copy...) from the remote server.
↓
11. Continue with the next task in the playbook.



