##) ANSIBLE CONFIGURATION
--------------------------

---> ansible.cfg

Definition:

*) ansible.cfg is a configuration file which contains the default settings or default configurations that control how Ansible should behave/configure itself.

*) We use ansible.cfg to define settings that we repeatedly use while working with Ansible instead of specifying them every time,
   we can configure them as defaults in ansible.cfg.

Sections in ansible.cfg:
------------------------

Ansible configuration file can contain different sections. Each section is used to group its related configuration settings.

[defaults] is one of the sections in ansible.cfg and contains the default settings for Ansible.

Example:
--------

[defaults]

inventory = ./inventory.ini
remote_user = ec2-user
private_key_file = ~/.ssh/roboshop.pem

Some commonly used sections are:
--------------------------------

*)Not every company project will have all of these sections. A project only includes the sections/settings based on their requirement.

[defaults]
→ General/default Ansible settings.

[privilege_escalation]
→ Controls how Ansible gets admin permissions on managed nodes.

[ssh_connection]
→ Settings related to SSH connections.

[persistent_connection]
→ Settings related to persistent connections.

[colors]
→ Settings related to Ansible output colors.


Common Settings In ansible.cfg:
-------------------------------

*)inventory
→ Defines the default inventory file/location.

Example:
inventory = ./inventory.ini


*)remote_user
→ Defines the default user Ansible uses to connect to managed nodes.

Example:
remote_user = ec2-user


*)private_key_file
→ Defines the default SSH private key Ansible uses to connect to managed nodes.

Example:
private_key_file = ~/.ssh/roboshop.pem


Ansible Configuration File Location:
------------------------------------


*) When you run a playbook, Ansible looks for the ansible.cfg configuration file in the following places.


1) ANSIBLE_CONFIG environment variable:

   Example:

   export ANSIBLE_CONFIG=/home/roboshop/custom-ansible.cfg

   → ANSIBLE_CONFIG is an environment variable that points to the configuration file.


2) ansible.cfg in the current directory:

   Example:

   /home/roboshop/ansible/ansible.cfg

   If we are currently inside:

   /home/roboshop/ansible/

   → Ansible can use the ansible.cfg present in the current directory.


3) ~/.ansible.cfg:  current user's home directory

   Example:

   /home/roboshop/.ansible.cfg

   Here:
   ~ → /home/roboshop

   → This is the ansible.cfg file in the current user's home directory.


4) /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg: System-level setting

   Example:

   /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg

   → This is the system-level Ansible configuration file.


Ansible Configuration file Precedence(preference order):
--------------------------------------------------------

*) When you run a playbook, Ansible looks for the ansible.cfg configuration file in the following order of preference.

ANSIBLE_CONFIG( environment variable ) - First place where ansible searches for ansible.cfg
      ↓
./ansible.cfg( current directory ) - Second place where ansible searches for ansible.cfg if it is not found in 'ANSIBLE_CONFIG(environment variable)'
      ↓
~/.ansible.cfg( home directory ) - Third place where ansible searches for ansible.cfg if it is not found in './ansible.cfg( current directory)'
      ↓
/etc/ansible/ansible.cfg - Fourth place where ansible searches for ansible.cfg if it is not found in ~/.ansible.cfg( home directory)

Ansible uses the first applicable configuration file it finds.

Core Memory:
------------

ANSIBLE_CONFIG → Current Directory → Current user Home Directory → /etc/ansible


Important:
----------

The settings in ansible.cfg are defaults. A different value can be provided explicitly which can override the defaults for that particular execution.

Simple memory:

ansible.cfg → How Ansible should behave/configure itself.

