##)DYNAMIC INVENTORY
--------------------

*) In dynamic inventory, we don't manually maintain the hosts in the
   inventory file.

*) Ansible gets the current hosts information dynamically from an
   external source.
   
*) Dynamic inventory is useful when servers are frequently created,
   terminated, or changed in cloud environments.


Example:

Today:

frontend → 3 servers

Tomorrow:

frontend → 6 servers

*)Instead of manually updating the inventory file, Ansible can get the current host information from the external source.


STATIC INVENTORY:
-----------------

*) In static inventory, we manually maintain the hosts/servers in the inventory file.

Example:

[frontend]
10.0.0.10
10.0.0.11

[mongodb]
10.0.0.20



##) HOW DYNAMIC INVENTORY PROVIDES HOST INFORMATION
---------------------------------------------------

*) Dynamic inventory can provide host information to Ansible through:

1) Inventory script - Old Method ( just for your knowledge ChatGpt covered this method)
2) Inventory plugin - Modern/Current Method - you can learn and follow this method as this is recommended


1) Inventory Script:
--------------------

*) The script queries the external source and provides the host information to Ansible.

External source
      ↓
Inventory script
      ↓
Ansible


2) Inventory Plugin:
--------------------

*) Modern Ansible uses inventory plugins to get host information dynamically from an external sources.

External source
      ↓
Inventory plugin
      ↓
Ansible


Example of AWS EC2 inventory plugin:

plugin: amazon.aws.aws_ec2
regions:
  - ap-south-1


*) Here:

plugin: amazon.aws.aws_ec2 → Tells Ansible to use the AWS EC2 inventory plugin.

regions:
  - ap-south-1
→ Specifies the AWS region from which Ansible should obtain EC2 host information.


1) Verify the Dynamic Inventory:
--------------------------------

*) This command is used to verify whether the dynamic inventory is generating
   the expected hosts and groups correctly.
   
