##)ANSIBLE FORKS:
-----------------

*)Forks define the maximum number of hosts Ansible can process in parallel at the same time.
  By default, Ansible can process 5 managed hosts in parallel. Ansible can process number of hosts parallelly like 10, 30 and 40 based on control node CPU, RAM, 
  network capacity and the environment.

*)The forks value is configured in ansible.cfg

Example:

[defaults]
forks = 3


Suppose the inventory contains 10 hosts:

Host 1
Host 2
Host 3
Host 4
Host 5
Host 6
Host 7
Host 8
Host 9
Host 10
 

If forks = 3:

Ansible can process a maximum of 3 hosts parallelly.

Initially:

Process 1 → Host 1   (running)
Process 2 → Host 2   (running)
Process 3 → Host 3   (running)

The remaining hosts wait:

Host 4
Host 5
Host 6
...
Host 10


Suppose Host 2 finishes first:

Process 1 → Host 1   (running)
Process 2 → FREE
Process 3 → Host 3   (running)

Now the free process can be used for the next waiting host:

Process 1 → Host 1   (running)
Process 2 → Host 4   (running)
Process 3 → Host 3   (running)

Here, still only 3 hosts being processed parallelly.


Therefore:

forks = 3
→ Maximum 3 hosts can be processed in parallel.
→ Remaining hosts wait for an available process.
→ When a process becomes free, the next waiting host can use it.
→ The number of simultaneously processed hosts never exceeds 3.


Important:
----------

*)It means Ansible can process up to 3 hosts concurrently as declared but you can give forks as 10,30 and 40 based on the control node CPU, RAM, 
  network capacity and the environment.

For fixed batches, Ansible has a separate concept called "serial".


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


##)ANSIBLE SERIAL:
------------------

*)Serial is used when we want Ansible to work on hosts in batches.
*)Serial is mainly useful for controlled or rolling deployments.
*)It controls how many hosts Ansible handles in one batch before moving to the next batch.


Example:

- hosts: webservers
  serial: 2

--> Assume that Suppose there are 10 web servers

Ansible handles them like:

Batch 1 → Server 1, Server 2
           ↓
        tasks complete
           ↓
Batch 2 → Server 3, Server 4
           ↓
        tasks complete
           ↓
Batch 3 → Server 5, Server 6
           ↓
        tasks complete
           ↓
Batch 4 → Server 7, Server 8
           ↓
        tasks complete
           ↓
Batch 5 → Server 9, Server 10


If one server finishes early in the current batch, Ansible does not bring another server into that batch, It waits until the current batch is 
completed before moving to the next batch.

Q)Why do we use serial?

A)Serial is mainly useful for controlled or rolling deployments.


Important Example and this can click the serial topic:
------------------------------------------------------

We have 10 production web servers and want to deploy a new
application version.

Instead of changing all 10 servers at once, if u set as below

serial: 2

→ Deploy to 2 servers and Check whether the application is working.
  If the current batch is healthy, Ansible automatically moves to the next 2 servers and Continue until all servers are updated.

→ If the current batch fails, the play can fail and the rollout can stop, so the remaining servers are not updated.


Example with health check:
--------------------------

- hosts: webservers
  serial: 2

  tasks:

    - name: Deploy application
      ansible.builtin.shell: ./deploy.sh

    - name: Check application health
      ansible.builtin.uri:
        url: "http://localhost/health"
        status_code: 200
      register: health_check

    - name: Fail if application is unhealthy
      ansible.builtin.fail:
        msg: "Application health check failed"
      when: health_check.status != 200


Flow:
-----

Batch 1 → Server 1, Server 2
             ↓
       Deploy application
             ↓
       Health check
             ↓
   If healthy → next batch
   If unhealthy → fail/stop rollout


There is no separate task to move to the next batch of hosts.
When the current batch of servers or hosts completes successfully, Ansible automatically moves to the next batch of servers.


Important:
----------

*)serial = controls the batch size.
*)forks = controls how many hosts Ansible can process in parallel.